# Publishing this app

This app is shared **Public** (no sign-in) so family can open it from a link.
Every publish must pass Cursor's security review as a public app.

## Before asking Kelven to publish

1. Self-review every shipped file against the review classes (missing auth,
   CSRF, XSS, injection, data exposure, resource exhaustion, insecure
   defaults, secret logging). Fix every finding, including low severity.
2. Build and run locally; exercise the PIN gate (wrong guesses lock out with
   429, the correct PIN is also refused while locked).
3. Commit, open a PR, **merge to `main`**, and pull `main`.
4. Only then call `publish-app` from `main`. Never ask Kelven to publish a
   branch build or before the merge.
5. If a publish reports review notes, fix them all in the same way before the
   next request — don't hand the notes back as optional.

## Edit access model — keep it simple

- Viewing is open; only writes need edit access.
- Edit access is a short shared family PIN (`EDIT_PIN`, 6–8 digits, Apps
  Secrets). Do not switch to a long password or accounts unless no PIN-based
  fix can pass review.
- Brute force is stopped server-side: one global attempt counter, 5 wrong
  guesses then a lockout that doubles each time (cap 1 hour), and every guess
  is refused while locked. Escalation resets after 24 quiet hours.
- Edit sessions last 90 days, and a device that unlocked once gets a
  trusted-device cookie with its own guess bucket, so a stranger tripping the
  global lockout cannot block family devices.
- No third-party requests from pages (system fonts only; no CDNs/analytics).
- Once a publish is requested, don't push to `main` until it finishes —
  changing the app mid-review cancels the public review.
- Every write route: `requireEditAuth`, same-origin check, small JSON body,
  bounded inputs, generic error messages.
- Keep security headers in `next.config.ts` (framing blocked, nosniff, HSTS).
